<!-- include: forge/stage6 -->

## Stage 7 - review

<!-- include: forge/stage7 -->

## After merge only

The pipeline ends at approval, not at merge. `merge_watch` is a low-frequency tail: keep
watching the <!-- include: vocab/short -->, and only once the merge is actually observed on the forge, write to the tracker: <!-- include: harness/tracker-close -->.
Only after the merge is observed, and always in plain informal English, not a formal template.

**This is enforced, not just a convention, only when `ppl doctor` reports the guard hook
registered** (`local`, or an accepted `global`/`both`) **for this session** - the hook denies
every Jira write tool unless the active run's stage is `merge_watch` [R-JIRA-GATE]
[R-GUARD-NOT-ARMED n/a]. If `ppl doctor` reported `none` or `unknown`, this section is a rule
you keep by discipline, not a cage: the run's stage being `merge_watch` or not makes no
difference to whether the write physically goes through, and `OWNER_DECISION` has nothing to
print because nothing ran a check to produce it [R-GUARD-NOT-ARMED n/a]. Check `ppl doctor`
before trusting either reading rather than assuming the more comfortable one. When it IS armed: if you somehow reach this point with the
run still in `review` or `closure`, the write will be denied outright (its reason starts with
`OWNER_DECISION`) - go back and run `ppl stage enter merge_watch` first, do not try the
write again hoping the hook missed it [R-JIRA-GATE]. This gate fires for the orchestrator's own session -
which carries no `agent_type` - whenever the run is active and `isolated` is true (the
default), regardless of the session's cwd; the documented worktree layout in Stage 1 puts
the worktree beside the orchestrator's session, not under it, so a cwd-only check would not
have caught this session in the first place. With `--no-isolated`, this falls back to
a cwd check instead (DESIGN 5.4).

Once the outcome is known, label the accepted SHA: `ppl label --sha <sha> --actual
correct|defective|outcompeted --task <TASK-ID>`. `outcompeted` is for a correct candidate
that a parallel implementation of the same task replaced; `ppl metrics` reports it as
`outcompeted_rate` so design losses are counted, not only defects [R-METRIC-OUTCOMPETED].

## Operator commands

The panel writes `control.json`. `ppl` checks it at `stage enter`, `brief set`,
`baseline record`, `boundary set`, `mr set`, `review approved`, `budget spend`, `transition`
and `recover`, and refuses to move a paused, stopped or destroyed run - read-only commands
like `status`, `show` and `control get` keep working, so a paused run is still observable,
and `control set resume` keeps working too, so the operator can lift their own halt [R-OPERATOR-HALT-CLI].
`ppl park` and `ppl escalate` also stay available while paused - an operator halt must not
block recording why the run stopped [R-OPERATOR-HALT-CLI]. When `ppl doctor` reports the guard hook registered, it also checks `control.json` on
every tool call, so a pause lands within one tool call, not one stage; for the orchestrator's
own session it does so under the same isolated-scoping rule as the Jira gate above. When it
is not registered, `ppl`'s own checks above are what a pause has - it still blocks the next
`ppl` call, just not the tool call the operator actually wanted stopped. If a run carries
`remote_cleanup.json`, the operator asked for a full destroy: close the <!-- include: vocab/noun -->,
delete the remote branch, then run `ppl destroy --scope local`. That is the only outward
mutation the panel can ask you for, and it only ever happens after the operator typed the
task id to confirm it.

## Hard rules

These are your own discipline first, and a cage second - the cage half only ever holds when
`ppl doctor` reported the guard hook registered for this session. Follow every rule below
regardless of what `ppl doctor` says; the marked ones are also backed by a hook when it is
armed, which catches an accident, not a determined attempt to route around this list.

- Do not resolve a <!-- include: vocab/noun --> thread, approve, or merge - when the hook is
  armed, the forge's matching tools are denied outright [R-MCP-RESOLVE-THREAD]
  [R-MCP-APPROVE-MR] [R-MCP-MERGE-MR]; when it is not, this is discipline only.
- Do not write to the tracker before the merge is observed, and do not write outside stage
  `merge_watch` - see "After merge only" above for exactly when this is hook-backed and
  when it is not.
- Do not edit a test to turn a gate green. This one has no hook check at all, armed or not -
  a gate is a receipt you record yourself, not a write the guard hook watches.
- Do not dispatch a worker to fix something outside its write boundary. When the hook is
  armed, the worker's own write attempt is also denied [R-ROLE-CODE-WORKER-BOUNDARY]; when
  it is not, dispatching it correctly was the only thing that stopped it.
- Do not trust a summary of build output. Read the exit-code file. This is not a hook check
  at all, armed or not - `ppl` has no way to see what you read.
- Do not run `git add -A`, a bare `git stash`, `git stash pop`, `git push --force`, or
  `git reset --hard`. **When `ppl doctor` reports the guard hook registered**, it denies all
  five outright, everywhere in that session; **when it does not**, none of the five is
  denied and this is exactly as unprotected as any other command on this list - see
  `references/gates.md` for why each one is dangerous here specifically [R-GIT-ADD-ALL] [R-GIT-STASH] [R-GIT-FORCE-PUSH] [R-GIT-RESET-HARD].
- If a budget is exhausted, park with a handoff. Do not quietly continue, and do not switch
  to another model to route around the limit. `ppl stage enter` and `ppl transition`
  themselves refuse past the hard cap regardless of the guard hook [R-BUDGET-DISPATCH-CAP]
  [R-BUDGET-RETRY] - it is the state machine, not the hook, that owns the budget.
